    "evidence_text": "Regulatory T (T reg) cells are critical regulators of immune tolerance.\n\nHowever, these markers have proven problematic for uniquely defining this specialized T cell subset in humans.\n\nThese cells were highly suppressive in functional suppressor assays.\n\nIn Vitro\u2013expanded Antigen-specific Regulatory T Cells Suppress Autoimmune Diabetes\n\nResponse to self antigen imprints regulatory memory in tissues\n\nIn fact, these diseases often spontaneously resolve, despite persistent tissue autoantigen expression.\n\nRESEARCH DESIGN AND METHODS\n\nThese different kinetics may suggest complementary clinical applications for T-cell and aAb measurements.\n\nType 1 diabetes-associated IL2RA variation lowers IL-2 signaling and contributes to diminished CD4+CD25+ regulatory T cell function.\n\nInterleukin-2 gene variation impairs regulatory T cell function and causes autoimmunity\n\nAutoimmune diseases are thought to result from imbalances in normal immune physiology and regulation.\n\nGenome-wide association studies are now identifying disease-associated chromosome regions.\n\nThese cells inhibited the responses of CD4+ cells to the mAb itself and to antigen.\n\nCytokine production by Teffs remained continually suppressed by Tregs.\n\nDepletion of Tregs significantly decreased mortality following cecal ligation and puncture.",
